I would maintain the original assessment. The conclusion does not coherently follow from the evidence as stated.

The evidence supports only the claim that students value timely feedback. The conclusion adds several unsupported assumptions:

- that automated tools provide feedback that is sufficiently useful or educational;
- that speed should outweigh other goals of writing courses;
- that writing courses are primarily or only about feedback;
- that replacement, rather than supplementation, is warranted.

A more defensible conclusion would be: “Because students value timely feedback, universities should explore ways to make feedback faster, including possible use of automated feedback tools.”

